.TH POWEROFF 8 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
poweroff \- stop the system and turn the machine off
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B poweroff
.RB [ \-f ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
By default
.B poweroff
sends
.B SIGUSR1
to
.BR init
(process 1), which performs an orderly shutdown (see
.BR halt (8))
and ends by calling
.BR reboot (2)
with
.BR RB_POWER_OFF .
.B poweroff
returns as soon as the signal has been delivered.
If signalling init fails,
.B poweroff
prints a warning and calls
.BR reboot (2)
itself.
.PP
The kernel does not parse ACPI tables.
To power off it writes the S5 sleep command to the power-control
ports used by QEMU (0x604), Bochs and older QEMU (0xB004) and
VirtualBox (0x4004).
If none of these takes effect, as on most real hardware, the CPU is
halted with interrupts disabled; the machine is never rebooted.
.PP
An effective user ID of 0 is required.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Do not signal init; call
.BR reboot (2)
with
.B RB_POWER_OFF
directly.
Processes are not terminated and
.B /etc/rc stop
is not run, although the kernel still unmounts all file systems.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Init was signalled.
.TP
.B 1
.BR reboot (2)
failed (for example with
.B EPERM
when not run as root).
.TP
.B 2
An unrecognized argument was given; a usage message is printed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kill (2),
.BR reboot (2),
.BR halt (8),
.BR reboot (8)
.SH BUGS
Power-off works only under the emulators listed above; on real
hardware the machine is left halted and must be switched off by hand.
